# containermanager

Direct Linux namespace launcher for explicit immutable projections and complete
mutable Fedora roots, with isolated homes, declared host integrations, and
lock-backed legacy package projections.

This is intentionally not a Podman/Distrobox runtime wrapper. Runtime profiles
use direct namespace and mount APIs and declare root semantics structurally;
there is no default root mode and no scalar `root = "..."` compatibility form.
Fedora roots are acquired directly through the OCI Distribution API.

## Mutable Fedora roots

A mutable Fedora profile names a complete Fedora root containing DNF5, RPM,
repository configuration, signing keys, and CA trust. DNF is not run from the
host and containermanager does not use `dnf --installroot`.

Import an OCI image into a new root atomically:

```sh
cargo run -- fedora-root-import \
  --image registry.fedoraproject.org/fedora:44 \
  --root /home/user/.local/state/containermanager/environments/fedora-agent/root
```

The importer resolves image indexes and manifests through the registry API,
selects the host Linux architecture, handles anonymous Bearer-token challenges,
verifies every manifest, config, and layer SHA-256 digest, and applies OCI layers
with whiteout semantics. It then enters the same subordinate-ID namespace used
by the runtime, restores numeric ownership and file capabilities, validates
Fedora, DNF5, RPM, and repository state, and publishes the root without replacing
an existing path. Source identity is recorded at
`/var/lib/containermanager/fedora-root-origin.json` inside the imported root,
including the requested reference, immutable config digest, manifest digest,
platform, media type, and canonical repository digest. Registry access and layer
application do not occur on sandbox creation or command dispatch.

```toml
name = "fedora-agent"
root = { kind = "mutable-fedora", path = "/home/user/.local/state/containermanager/environments/fedora-agent/root", dnf_cache = "/home/user/.cache/containermanager/dnf/fedora44" }
shell = ["/bin/bash", "-l"]
```

The root is entered as writable `/`. Namespace root maps to the caller, while
namespace IDs 1 through 65535 map through the caller's `/etc/subuid` and
`/etc/subgid` ranges using `newuidmap` and `newgidmap`. This allows normal RPM
transactions to create and own files as Fedora system users. The declared DNF5
cache is mounted read-write at `/var/cache/libdnf5`.

Run the Fedora root's own package manager normally:

```sh
cargo run -- run --profile examples/fedora-agent.toml -- dnf5 install -y ripgrep
```

The opt-in end-to-end test uses `fedora-root-import`, installs nginx through
Fedora's DNF5, exercises sysusers and subordinate ownership, tests a nested user
namespace through the broker, tears the sandbox down, re-enters it, and verifies
that the RPM transaction persisted:

```sh
crates/containermanager/tests/e2e_mutable_fedora.sh
```

## Embedding and durable sandboxes

The `containermanager` crate exposes a process-owning library API for agent
harnesses. It does not enter namespaces, call `fork()` for namespace setup, or
replace the embedding process. `SandboxManager` starts the dedicated
`containermanager-sandbox-launcher` executable, which prepares one sandbox and
keeps its namespaces and mounts alive until the handle is closed.

Launcher ownership is tracked with an inherited lifetime pipe. The embedding process keeps the
write end in `DurableSandbox`; EOF tells the namespace service that the owner process or handle is
gone. This deliberately avoids `PR_SET_PDEATHSIG` for the launcher because Linux ties that signal
to the specific thread that created the child, which is unsafe for thread-pool embedders.

```rust
use containermanager::{DurableSandboxSpec, ExecRequest, SandboxManager};

let manager = SandboxManager::new("/absolute/path/to/containermanager-sandbox-launcher")?;
let sandbox = manager.start(DurableSandboxSpec::new(
    "/absolute/path/to/fedora-root",
    "/absolute/path/to/workspace",
    "/absolute/path/to/dnf-cache",
))?;

let output = sandbox.exec_capture(ExecRequest::new(vec![
    "/usr/bin/git".into(),
    "status".into(),
    "--short".into(),
]))?;
```

Sandbox preparation occurs once. Each dispatch connects to a private Unix
socket, passes caller-owned stdin/stdout/stderr or PTY descriptors with
`SCM_RIGHTS`, and forks only inside the already prepared single-threaded
launcher environment. Multiple commands can execute concurrently. Command
handles support signal delivery and explicit waiting; closing the sandbox tears
down the namespace holder and its descendants.

### Cgroup v2 command containment

The durable API can keep the namespace-holding launcher separate from resource-limited jobs. The
embedding process must own a delegated, writable cgroup v2 parent. With systemd, run it using
`Delegate=yes` and `DelegateSubgroup=supervisor`; then the parent can be derived automatically:

```rust
use containermanager::{
    CgroupConfig, CgroupLimits, CpuMax, DurableSandboxSpec, SandboxManager,
};

let limits = CgroupLimits::new()
    .with_memory_high(6 * 1024 * 1024 * 1024)
    .with_memory_max(8 * 1024 * 1024 * 1024)
    .with_pids_max(512)
    .with_cpu_max(CpuMax::new(400_000, 100_000)?);
let cgroup = CgroupConfig::from_current_process_parent(limits)?;
let spec = DurableSandboxSpec::new(root, workspace, dnf_cache).with_cgroup(cgroup);
let sandbox = SandboxManager::new(launcher)?.start(spec)?;
```

The resulting hierarchy has a protected `service/` leaf and a constrained `jobs/` branch with one
`job-N/` leaf per command. Limits apply both to each job and to all concurrent jobs in aggregate.
Hard memory limits default to `memory.swap.max=0` unless an explicit swap limit is supplied. Memory
OOM and PID exhaustion are returned through `ExitOutcome::resource_limit`; cleanup uses
`cgroup.kill` and removes the complete sandbox subtree.

The durable API requires an already acquired Fedora root. Registry access is
confined to the explicit `fedora-root-import` acquisition command and is not part
of sandbox creation or command dispatch.

## Platform sandbox status

The one-shot sandbox is implemented with native platform primitives and fails closed where a backend is incomplete:

- **Linux:** namespaces, empty-root mount isolation, seccomp, cgroup v2, pidfds, Unix capability FDs, Unix sockets, and sealed memfds are implemented and tested.
- **Windows:** the library requires `Experimental_CreateProcessInSandbox` from `processmodel.dll`; there is no ordinary `CreateProcess` fallback. The BaseContainer spec enables AppContainer isolation and Win32k blocking, leaves capabilities/network policy empty, and exposes only explicit read-only/read-write filesystem grants. Containermanager refuses launch unless those grants cover the worker executable and effective current directory; other host filesystem paths remain default-deny. The worker is attached to a kill-on-close Job Object with memory/process-count/optional CPU limits before it is resumed. General handle inheritance remains disabled. Decoder IPC uses host-created named objects ACL'd to the unique worker AppContainer SID: read-only input mappings, read/write output mappings, and a single-client duplex control pipe in the `\\.\pipe\LOCAL\...` namespace.
- **macOS:** the crate compiles so applications can keep their platform dispatch intact, but `MacosSandboxManager::check_support()`, the CLI, and the sandbox launcher all fail closed until a separately signed XPC/App Sandbox backend exists. There is no direct-process or unsandboxed fallback.

Windows named mappings are the capability bridge required by BaseContainer's no-handle-inheritance rule; mapping names are locators rather than secrets, with the DACL providing authorization. macOS will use a separately signed XPC worker rather than attempting unsupported dynamic sandboxing of arbitrary executables.

## One-shot process sandboxes

For native helpers that should be treated as compromised from startup, use the
one-shot process API instead of a durable Fedora environment. `ProcessSandboxSpec`
starts from an empty tmpfs root, creates fresh user, PID, network, IPC, UTS, and
mount namespaces by default, clears the inherited environment, drops namespace
capabilities, sets `no_new_privs`, and does not expose the containermanager broker.
The worker program must be an absolute path inside the sandbox.

```rust
use std::fs::File;
use std::io::{Read, Write};
use std::os::fd::OwnedFd;
use containermanager::{
    CgroupConfig, CgroupLimits, ProcessSandboxIo, ProcessSandboxSpec, SandboxManager,
    SeccompAction, SeccompPolicy, SharedMemory,
};

let limits = CgroupLimits::new()
    .with_memory_max(512 * 1024 * 1024)
    .with_memory_swap_max(0)
    .with_pids_max(16);
let cgroup = CgroupConfig::from_current_process_parent(limits)?;
let seccomp = SeccompPolicy::denylist(
    [
        libc::SYS_ptrace,
        libc::SYS_mount,
        libc::SYS_umount2,
        libc::SYS_unshare,
        libc::SYS_setns,
    ],
    SeccompAction::KillProcess,
)?;

let input: OwnedFd = File::open("/absolute/path/to/untrusted-image")?.into();
let output = SharedMemory::new(64 * 1024 * 1024)?;
let mut io = ProcessSandboxIo::inherit()?;
let input_fd = io.pass_fd(input)?;
let (mut control, control_fd) = io.socket_pair()?;
let output_fd = io.pass_shared_memory(&output)?;

let spec = ProcessSandboxSpec::new("/app/bin/decoder")
    .arg(format!("--input-fd={input_fd}"))
    .arg(format!("--control-fd={control_fd}"))
    .arg(format!("--output-fd={output_fd}"))
    .read_only_bind("/absolute/path/to/decoder-runtime", "/app")
    .tmpfs("/tmp")
    .with_cgroup(cgroup)
    .with_seccomp(seccomp);

let child = SandboxManager::new(launcher)?.spawn_process(spec, io)?;
control.write_all(b"decode\n")?;
let mut done = [0; 2];
control.read_exact(&mut done)?;
let outcome = child.wait()?;

Passing an already-open descriptor is preferred to binding the untrusted input
path into the sandbox. `ProcessSandboxIo::pass_fd` assigns an unused worker-side
descriptor and returns a `SandboxFd` token suitable for the worker protocol or
command line; `with_fd` remains available when an exact descriptor number is required.
`socket_pair` creates a duplex Unix stream with only the peer endpoint inherited by
the worker. `SharedMemory` uses a fixed-size anonymous memfd: both sides can read and
write its contents, but the worker cannot resize it or change its seals. This is useful
for keeping small control messages on the socket while decoded image/video buffers stay
out of the byte-stream protocol. For small generated inputs or metadata that must never
be modified, `ImmutableMemory::from_bytes` creates a fully sealed memfd and
`ProcessSandboxIo::pass_immutable_memory` passes a duplicate capability to the worker.

`ProcessSandboxSpec::with_seccomp` compiles an architecture-checked classic-BPF policy
internally on x86-64 and AArch64; callers can use an allowlist or a default-allow
denylist, while `ProcessSandboxIo::with_seccomp_filter` remains available for externally
generated filters. The example denylist is only defense-in-depth, not a complete
media-decoder syscall policy. Cgroup limits cover the launcher and its complete worker
process tree; memory and PID limit events are reported through
`ExitOutcome::resource_limit`. `ProcessSandboxChild::wait_timeout` uses a pidfd to wait
for a wall-clock deadline without terminating the worker, so the embedding application
can explicitly `kill()` a decoder that exceeds its deadline.

Linux `ProcessSandboxChild` and Windows `WindowsSandboxChild` both implement the portable `OneShotSandboxChild` lifecycle trait, and their outcomes implement `OneShotSandboxExit`. `wait_or_kill(child, deadline)` provides a shared deadline policy: it waits without killing first, tolerates an exit racing the deadline, then terminates and reaps the contained process tree if the worker is still running. Launch configuration and IPC intentionally remain platform-native.

## Windows BaseContainer workers

On Windows, use the native BaseContainer manager. The worker environment is empty by default, its current directory defaults to the executable's parent directory, and filesystem access must be granted explicitly. Validation fails before process creation unless a read-only or read/write grant covers both the executable and the effective current directory. Device/verbatim path namespaces and parent traversal are rejected, and overlapping read-only/read-write grant trees are rejected rather than relying on an ambiguous precedence rule. The default Job Object permits only one active process. General handle inheritance is always disabled.

`WindowsSandboxManager::check_support()` can be used before preparing worker IPC to verify that `processmodel.dll` is present and exports `Experimental_CreateProcessInSandbox`. It is only an API-availability probe; actual sandbox creation can still fail because of host policy or requested Job limits.
When using `spawn_with_io`, call `WindowsSandboxSpec::validate()` before `WindowsSandboxIo::new()` when practical so invalid path/resource policy is rejected before a temporary AppContainer profile is allocated. The simpler `spawn()` path performs that preflight automatically.

For a real Windows validation run from PowerShell, use `crates/containermanager/scripts/windows-smoke.ps1`. It prints the Windows/Rust versions, compile-checks all containermanager targets, verifies the experimental BaseContainer export first, then runs the ignored BaseContainer integration regressions serially to avoid resource-test interference.

```rust
use std::time::Duration;
use containermanager::{
    WindowsJobLimits, WindowsSandboxIo, WindowsSandboxManager, WindowsSandboxSpec,
};

WindowsSandboxManager::check_support()?;

let mut io = WindowsSandboxIo::new()?;
let input = io.immutable_memory(image_bytes)?;
let mut output = io.shared_memory(64 * 1024 * 1024)?;
let mut control = io.control_pipe()?;

let spec = WindowsSandboxSpec::new(r"C:\decoder\worker.exe")
    .arg(format!("--input-mapping={}", input.name()))
    .arg(format!("--input-len={}", input.len()))
    .arg(format!("--output-mapping={}", output.name()))
    .arg(format!("--output-len={}", output.len()))
    .arg(format!("--control-pipe={}", control.name()))
    .read_only_path(r"C:\decoder")
    .with_env("DECODER_MODE", "sandboxed")
    .with_job_limits(
        WindowsJobLimits::new()
            .with_job_memory_bytes(512 * 1024 * 1024)
            .with_active_process_limit(1)
            .with_cpu_rate_percent(50)
            .with_job_user_time_limit(Duration::from_secs(2)),

let mut child = WindowsSandboxManager::new().spawn_with_io(spec, io)?;
if !control.connect_timeout(Duration::from_secs(2))? {
    child.kill()?;
    let _ = child.wait()?;
    anyhow::bail!("decoder did not connect its control pipe");
}
if !control.write_all_timeout(b"decode\n", Duration::from_secs(2))? {
    child.kill()?;
    anyhow::bail!("decoder stopped reading its control pipe");
}
let mut done = [0; 5];
if !control.read_exact_timeout(&mut done, Duration::from_secs(2))? {
    child.kill()?;
    anyhow::bail!("decoder stopped responding on its control pipe");
}
let outcome = child.wait()?;
if let Some(usage) = outcome.resource_usage {
    eprintln!(
        "decoder peak job memory: {} bytes across {} process(es)",
        usage.peak_job_memory_bytes,
        usage.total_processes,
    );
}

let mut decoded_header = [0; 16];
output.read_at(0, &mut decoded_header)?;
```

`WindowsImmutableMemory` grants the worker only `FILE_MAP_READ`; `WindowsSharedMemory` grants read/write access and keeps a host mapping for positional copies. The named-object DACL contains the per-worker AppContainer SID, so knowing a mapping or pipe name is not sufficient to open it from an unrelated sandbox. The control pipe is created under the AppContainer-compatible `\\.\pipe\LOCAL\...` namespace and its connection handshake has a caller-supplied timeout.

Each `WindowsSandboxIo` owns a fresh AppContainer SID, so named capabilities are isolated between concurrent workers as well as from the host. Learning another worker's mapping name does not grant access. If prepared IPC is abandoned before launch, `WindowsSandboxIo::cleanup()` explicitly deletes its temporary AppContainer profile and reports deletion failures; ordinary `Drop` remains best-effort cleanup.
The host-side `WindowsControlPipe` remains in nonblocking mode after connection. Use `read_exact_timeout` and `write_all_timeout` for protocol messages so a compromised worker cannot pin an embedding thread inside a synchronous pipe operation indefinitely; the raw `Read`/`Write` implementations return `WouldBlock` when no progress can be made immediately.

The environment block is always explicit and double-NUL-terminated; containermanager does not retry a failed launch with a `NULL` environment because that would inherit the embedding process environment. The Windows backend intentionally exposes only the currently verified read-only/read-write BaseContainer filesystem grants; it does not synthesize an undocumented explicit-deny field.
`WindowsJobLimits::with_cpu_rate_percent` applies a hard Job Object CPU-rate cap for the complete worker tree. `with_job_user_time_limit` separately applies an aggregate user-mode CPU-time budget whose explicit end-of-job action terminates the entire Job when exhausted. Requested limits are fail-closed: if Windows rejects their Job configuration, sandbox creation returns an error instead of launching with weaker limits. Wall-clock deadlines remain separate through `WindowsSandboxChild::wait_timeout`.
`WindowsExitOutcome::resource_usage` reports Job Object accounting such as peak per-process/job memory, total process count, and kernel/user CPU time. `WindowsExitOutcome::job_events` additionally exposes best-effort completion-port observations for active-process, job-memory, process-memory, and abnormal-exit events. Windows does not guarantee delivery of ordinary hard-limit Job completion messages, so absence of an event is not proof that the corresponding limit was never encountered; accounting and exit status remain authoritative for what they actually report.

BaseContainer `ui_restrictions` are intentionally left at zero. The worker is first placed in the SandboxEngine's Job and then assigned to containermanager's outer resource Job; Windows only forms that nested Job hierarchy when neither Job has UI limits. Headless UI attack-surface reduction is provided by `disallow_win32k_system_calls` instead.

A fresh AppContainer profile also has private per-app folder/registry storage that the AppContainer can access. Containermanager deletes the unique profile after the worker tree exits, but it does not currently impose a byte quota on that private profile storage. Treat disk-fill through private AppContainer storage as a remaining Windows resource-DoS consideration.

A Windows decoder worker can use the matching helper types rather than calling Win32 APIs directly:

```rust
use std::io::{Read, Write};
use std::time::Duration;
use containermanager::{
    WindowsWorkerControlPipe, WindowsWorkerReadOnlyMemory, WindowsWorkerSharedMemory,
};

let input = WindowsWorkerReadOnlyMemory::open(input_mapping_name, input_len)?;
let mut output = WindowsWorkerSharedMemory::open(output_mapping_name, output_len)?;
let mut control = WindowsWorkerControlPipe::connect_timeout(
    control_pipe_name,
    Duration::from_secs(2),
)?
.ok_or_else(|| anyhow::anyhow!("control pipe connection timed out"))?;

let mut request = [0; 7];
control.read_exact(&mut request)?;
// Decode from `input.read_at(...)`, write frames with `output.write_at(...)`.
control.write_all(b"ok")?;
```

The worker-side input helper requests only `FILE_MAP_READ`; the output helper requests read/write access. Both mapping helpers validate containermanager's `Local\\cm.*` namespace, and the control helper only accepts containermanager `\\\\.\\pipe\\LOCAL\\cm.*` names.

The repository includes ignored Windows-only integration tests that exercise the real experimental launcher, AppContainer-scoped control/memory capabilities, default filesystem/network denial, the Job active-process limit, timeout/kill, and process-tree cleanup. Run them on a supported Windows host with:

```powershell
cargo test -p containermanager --test windows_basecontainer -- --ignored
```

## Legacy Arch maintenance and package projections


Bootstrap a shared Arch maintenance root for package manifests:

```sh
state="${XDG_STATE_HOME:-$HOME/.local/state}/containermanager"
work="${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/containermanager-arch-bootstrap"
root="$state/roots/arch-maintenance/current"
mkdir -p "$work" "$root"
curl --fail --location --output "$work/archlinux-bootstrap-x86_64.tar.zst" \
  https://geo.mirror.pkgbuild.com/iso/latest/archlinux-bootstrap-x86_64.tar.zst
curl --fail --location --output "$work/sha256sums.txt" \
  https://geo.mirror.pkgbuild.com/iso/latest/sha256sums.txt
(cd "$work" && rg 'archlinux-bootstrap-x86_64\.tar\.zst$' sha256sums.txt | sha256sum -c -)
tar --zstd --extract --file "$work/archlinux-bootstrap-x86_64.tar.zst" \
  --directory "$root" --strip-components=1 \
  --no-same-owner --no-same-permissions --delay-directory-restore
chmod u+rwx "$root/dev" "$root/proc" "$root/run" "$root/sys"
mkdir -p "$state/prefixes/microkernel"/{home,shared,tmp}
```

Package installs in this single-ID user namespace need explicit rootfs
maintenance after bootstrap: initialize and populate the pacman keyring, enable
chosen mirrors in `/etc/pacman.d/mirrorlist`, and disable pacman's
`DownloadUser = alpm` setting because this namespace cannot chown download
directories to the `alpm` uid/gid. That is rootfs setup state, not a
launch-time integration policy.

