In the package-lock commands above, `--package-file-dir` and `--artifact-dir`
name filename-addressed source directories for already-downloaded artifacts.
They are local artifact source inputs only, not package-lock content identity
or runtime identity.
`--package-artifact-cache-root` and manifest `--package-cache-root` name the
content-addressed local package artifact cache.

The `plan` command renders the mount/namespace plan only. Every profile declares
an explicit tagged root. `read-only-projection` enters a caller-identity user
namespace and pivots into an immutable application projection.
`mutable-fedora` enters a subordinate-ID user namespace, makes the complete
Fedora root writable `/`, preserves its package-manager state, mounts the
profile home/binds plus runtime `/proc`, `/dev`, `/run`, `/tmp`, and `/sys`, and
executes the supplied command or profile shell. Profile filesystem access and

policy-selected host integration are documented with TCB exposure; see
[`docs/policies.md`](docs/policies.md). `network = "host"` shares the host
network namespace. `network = "proxy"` starts the guest in a private network
namespace and attaches `containermanager-networkproxy` from a host-network
supervisor. `resolver = "guest"` leaves the rootfs resolver in place and disables
proxy DNS/search advertisement. `resolver = "host"` projects host resolver
policy explicitly; with `network = "proxy"`, containermanager writes a generated
guest `/etc/resolv.conf` that uses proxy DNS-only forwarding to the host resolver
without enabling host gateway mapping. Containermanager owns proxy startup,
readiness, shutdown, and reaping. Nested `bwrap --unshare-net` creates a
separate offline network namespace; it is not attached to the proxy. Separately,
`host_network_config = "read-only"` binds host `/etc/hosts` and
`/etc/hostname`. The launcher also pins `SSL_CERT_FILE` and `SSL_CERT_DIR` to
the guest Arch trust store so host distro TLS paths do not leak into guest
commands. `host = "minimal"` under `[policy.environment]` avoids inheriting host
secrets and socket paths by default.

Package-aware app definitions are lock-backed package manifests, not a package
list bolted onto an ordinary runtime profile. A package manifest declares root
package requests and a manifest-relative package lock. Shared mutable package
manager roots are maintenance inputs used to generate or refresh locks; they are
not runtime manifest backends.

`examples/manifests/firefox-nightly-lock.toml` and
`examples/manifests/locked-app.toml` demonstrate `packages.backend = "lock"`.
The writable host home derives from the handwritten `[prefix].path`; `[home]`
declares only the guest path. Package realization reads exact artifacts from the
caller-selected content-addressed package cache.
`examples/manifests/locked-app.lock.toml`.
The checked-in package lock is a schema-valid documentation fixture with
illustrative checksums and sizes; real realization requires the exact package
artifacts named by the package lock to be present in the package artifact
cache. A manifest carries both sandbox rules (`network`, resolver, sockets,
home, binds, command) and root package requests needed to build the projected
runtime. Shared-Arch manifests resolve those requests against the shared Arch
maintenance root; lock-backed manifests validate them against the declared
package lock. Package-manager tools,
configuration, database state, caches, hooks, and keyrings remain maintenance
authority outside materialized runtimes. Shared-Arch manifests keep pacman
authority in the shared Arch maintenance root; lock-backed manifests filter
package-manager authority out of the exact locked closure instead of turning it
into runtime package-manager state. App runtimes consume realized projections.
Package-manager runtime authority is reserved
syntax and is rejected until its runtime database/cache/keyring model exists.
Maintenance-mode manifests also reject
profile-declared command entrypoints and mounts that would expose or invoke
those package-manager authorities through home, bind, socket, device,
parent-directory, or Arch merged-`/usr` alias paths. Package-manifest filesystem
paths and path-like command entrypoints must be canonical absolute paths.
Package locks are source-extensible reproducibility records for package
manifests. The current serialized package-lock source variants are
`rpm-repository` and `pacman-repository`; archive or snapshot mirrors are
repository source context plus exact package artifact URLs, sizes, and SHA-256
checksums, not separate hidden source variants. Locks also store
version-independent package-lock root identities, the selected package closure,
source-native dependency and provider metadata, and canonical installed path
lists, which are the package metadata a lock-backed materializer needs after
artifact retrieval.
Package source ids are unique within a lock. Every package-lock root identity
and locked package source field must reference an existing source record, and
each root/package format must match the referenced source record's package
format. Missing, duplicate, or format-mismatched source records make
`package-lock-check` fail before any cache or runtime materialization step.
Root/closure consistency is checked inside the lock itself: every `[[roots]]`
entry must be present in `[[packages]]` as exactly one selected package version
for that `(format, source, name, architecture)` package-lock root identity. The
selected package closure rejects duplicate root identities, duplicate locked
package identities, and multiple installed versions for the same package-lock
root identity, so a lock-backed runtime has one deterministic package artifact
record for each package identity.
`package-lock-check`
validates a package lock, computes a canonical
`package-lock-sha256-...` content id, and prints a deterministic summary of
package-lock source records, root identities, locked packages, and installed
paths without resolving mutable live repository metadata. The content id is
independent of TOML table ordering and dependency/provider/file-list ordering.
It is metadata-only: it does not accept `--package-artifact-cache-root`,
`--artifact-dir`, or `--package-source-root`, does not read package artifact
caches, and does not materialize package source roots.
Validation rejects contradictory package artifact identities: one URL cannot
name multiple checksum/size pairs, and one checksum cannot claim multiple sizes.
Unknown package-lock TOML fields are rejected instead of ignored, so freshness,
update, security, and source-selection policy must remain outside the package
lock schema.
Package-lock command `--lock` paths must name existing regular package lock
TOML files without symlink components.
They are local file-read inputs and diagnostics labels only; package-lock
content identity is computed from canonical lock contents, not from the path,
and the path is not runtime identity.
`package-lock-cache-check` verifies the same package lock against a
content-addressed local package artifact cache. The package artifact cache root
must be an existing absolute package artifact cache directory path without
symlink components, with an existing `sha256/` digest directory, and a package
with checksum `sha256:<hex>` must exist as a non-symlink regular file at
`<package-artifact-cache-root>/sha256/<hex>` with the locked size and checksum.
Cache check is verification-only: it does not accept `--artifact-dir` or
`--package-source-root`, does not import package artifacts, and does not
materialize package source roots.
The package artifact cache root is a local verification input only; package-lock
content identity is computed from canonical lock contents, not from the cache
path, and the path is not runtime identity.
`package-lock-cache-import` populates that package artifact cache from a local
source directory of already-downloaded package artifacts. Each locked package
artifact URL contributes its final filename segment; the command reads
`<artifact-dir>/<filename>` as a regular non-symlink package artifact file,
verifies the locked size and SHA-256 checksum, then stores it at the
content-addressed package artifact cache digest path through an adjacent
temporary file.
Cache import is cache-population only: it does not accept
`--package-source-root` and does not extract artifacts into package source
roots.
The package artifact cache root is a local cache-population input only, not
package-lock content identity or runtime identity.
The temporary package artifact file is verified and synced before it is
hard-linked into the final digest path without replacing existing files, and the
digest directory is synced after temp cleanup. The package artifact source
directory must be an existing absolute directory path without symlink
components. That source directory is a local artifact source input only, not
package-lock content identity or runtime identity. The package artifact cache
root is an absolute directory path without symlink components, and the missing
package artifact cache root directory or `sha256/` digest directory is created
without following symlinks.
This import path is source-extensible because it uses the locked package
artifact URL, size, and checksum fields common to every locked package record.
`package-lock-generate-pacman` creates a pacman package lock from a package
manifest, the pacman local database under `--arch-root`, and filename-addressed
regular non-symlink pacman package artifacts under `--package-file-dir`. The
command resolves the manifest `packages.include` closure from that pacman local
database, records the supplied pacman-repository source id, repo, base URL,
architecture, and snapshot as repository source context, and forms exact locked
package artifact URLs by appending package filenames to `--artifact-url-base` or
`--base-url`, writes an output package lock TOML, and can populate an absolute
content-addressed package artifact cache with `--package-artifact-cache-root`
using the same temp-verified, synced, no-replace digest entry installation as
`package-lock-cache-import`; the missing package artifact cache root directory
or `sha256/` digest directory is created without following symlinks. The
package file directory is a filename-addressed pacman artifact source for
pacman generation/refresh and optional cache population, not the
content-addressed package artifact cache root, package-lock content identity,
or runtime identity.
Pacman generation and refresh use `--package-file-dir` for filename-addressed
pacman artifacts; they reject generic `--artifact-dir` and
`--package-source-root`, which belong to cache import and source-root
realization.
For these direct package-lock commands, `--manifest` supplies
`packages.include` root package request names only; any manifest-declared
`[packages] lock` path is not read or written. `package-lock-generate-pacman`
writes `--output`, and `package-lock-refresh-pacman` reads `--lock` and writes
`--output`.
The `--output` path is a local package-lock file-install destination only; the
package-lock content id is computed from canonical lock contents, not from that
path, and the path is not runtime identity.
The recorded base URL and snapshot are repository source context; those package
artifact URLs, sizes, and checksums remain authoritative for reproduction. The
output path must not already exist, and its parent path, when specified, must be
an existing directory without symlink components, so the package-lock generation
command cannot overwrite an older rollback package lock; the final package lock
is installed from an adjacent temporary file so the output path is never a
partial TOML file.
The temporary file is synced before installation, and the parent directory is
synced after the final no-replace install and temp cleanup. It snapshots the
selected Arch root's pacman local database state into a package lock and, when
requested, populates a package artifact cache; it is not an online resolver and
does not decide freshness, update, security, or source-selection policy.
`package-lock-refresh-pacman` refreshes a pacman package lock from a
deliberately selected Arch root's pacman local database state. It reads an
existing package lock, requires exactly one pacman-repository source record and
only pacman packages in that package lock, reuses that pacman repository source
context (source id, repo, base URL, architecture, and snapshot), resolves the
manifest root package requests against the pacman local database under
`--arch-root`, and writes an output package lock using the same no-clobber output
invariant.
The package artifact URL base is inferred only when every existing pacman
package artifact URL has the same directory; otherwise pass
`--artifact-url-base` explicitly.
That URL base forms exact refreshed package artifact URLs and is not a resolver
or source-selection policy. With `--package-artifact-cache-root`, refresh uses
the same temp-verified digest entry installation as package-lock generation and
creates the missing package artifact cache root directory or `sha256/` digest
directory without following symlinks.
This is an operator-triggered snapshot/update workflow and does not make
freshness, update, security, or source-selection policy part of the package lock.
`package-lock-source-root-realize` extracts verified package artifacts from the
package artifact cache into a new absolute package source root and rejects
payload paths not recorded in the package lock metadata. The package artifact
cache root must be an existing absolute package artifact cache directory path
without symlink components and with an existing `sha256/` digest directory.
The package source root must not already exist, and its parent must be an
existing directory path without symlink components; a partially created package
source root is removed on extraction or validation failure. After verification,
each package artifact cache file is reopened without following symlinks before
decompression and extraction. The package source root is local extraction
output, not a package-lock source variant, package artifact cache,
package-lock content identity, or runtime identity.
Source-root realization reads only the content-addressed package artifact cache;
it does not accept `--artifact-dir`, so filename-addressed source directories
remain cache-import and package-lock generation inputs.
Extraction supports pacman `.pkg.tar.zst` package artifacts and RPM package
artifacts with zstd-compressed `cpio` payloads.
The package crate has lock-native inventory/projection building blocks that
preserve package-lock root identities `(format, source, name, architecture)`
separately from source-native version, dependency, provider, and installed-path
metadata. The package store can materialize lock-native projections from a
materialized package source root and persist and read the matching lock-native
inventory/projection metadata. It can also activate a lock-native projection by
package-lock content id and bind runtime identity metadata. That content id is
stored in the lock-native projection `inventory_id` field, so an existing root
cannot be relabeled with a different runtime `package_source` identity or
manifest package declaration.
For `packages.backend = "lock"`, `manifest-realize` requires
`--package-cache-root <dir>` and uses the manifest-declared package lock plus
that package artifact cache as local package realization inputs to build and
activate the runtime projection without reading the shared Arch maintenance
root's pacman local database.
The package artifact cache root passed via `--package-cache-root` must be an
existing absolute package artifact cache directory path without symlink
components and with an existing `sha256/` digest directory.
The shorter `--package-cache-root` spelling is the manifest-realization and
`activate` flag; direct package-lock commands use
`--package-artifact-cache-root` for the same local package artifact cache
layout and reject the manifest `--package-cache-root` spelling.
Lock-backed package manifests use a compound projection key derived from the
package-lock content ID, exact selected projection, package declaration, package
manager policy, and scaffold paths. Mixed-format projection consumes the exact
closure recorded in the package lock; it does not solve dependencies across
formats or reinterpret one source's dependency language as another's.

Package manifests require `packages.backend = "lock"` with
`lock = "app.lock.toml"` inside `[packages]`. The lock reference is a normal
manifest-relative path. The manifest and lock are opened beneath one pinned
manifest-parent descriptor with no symlink traversal below that parent;
symlinked user-path ancestors such as Fedora Atomic's `/home -> /var/home` are
accepted. `--state-root` does not rewrite the lock reference or derive a package
artifact cache root.

`manifest-plan`, `manifest-realize`, `manifest-rollback`, `manifest-verify`,
`manifest-run`, `activate`, `manifest-generations`, `manifest-pin`,
`manifest-unpin`, and `manifest-gc` are lock-only package-manifest commands.
root names to match `packages.include` by name; source, format, and architecture
remain authoritative package-lock data. `manifest-plan` does not accept
realization inputs or read the package artifact cache.

Runtime identity records the package-lock content ID and the exact compound
projection key. The serialized `package_source` field is an admission
discriminator, not a repository source record, cache path, or temporary package
source root. An existing projection cannot be relabeled with a different lock,
package declaration, projection, or scaffold identity.

Package commands default state to `$XDG_STATE_HOME/containermanager`, or
`$HOME/.local/state/containermanager` when `XDG_STATE_HOME` is unset.
`--state-root` rebases manifest state placeholders and derived prefix paths; it
does not rewrite the manifest-relative lock reference or package artifact cache.

`manifest-realize` requires `--package-cache-root`. It verifies package
artifacts, extracts them into a temporary source root, materializes the exact
lock projection and typed scaffold paths into a staged generation, and writes:

- strict lock-native inventory and projection metadata
- strict runtime identity metadata
- a complete projection integrity manifest with file hashes, sizes, modes,
  directory modes, and symlink targets
- compact raw-lock admission metadata

The staged generation is committed with no-replace semantics and selected
through the activation symlink. The projection key hash domain is versioned, so
a format change creates a new generation identity rather than interpreting old
metadata through a compatibility path.

`activate` first derives the exact desired generation and validates an existing
one before requiring `--package-cache-root`. If absent, it requires the cache
and realizes it. Corrupt, malformed, or inaccessible existing generations are
reported as errors rather than rebuilt or overwritten. After selection,
`activate` enters the exact projection root returned by activation.

`manifest-run` is the fast admission path. It hashes the exact raw lock bytes,
reads compact runtime identity and lock-admission metadata, validates manifest
declaration and scaffold identity, and enters the exact selected projection
root. It does not parse the package lock, deserialize full projection metadata,
walk the projection tree, or read a package artifact cache.
A text-only lock edit that preserves the canonical package-lock content ID still
requires explicit `activate` or `manifest-realize`. Explicit activation reuses
the completely validated generation and atomically refreshes its raw-lock
admission evidence under the activation lock; it does not copy package payload.

`manifest-verify` is the explicit complete verification path. It parses the
lock, derives the exact projection identity, requires that exact generation to
be selected, validates strict metadata and all projected paths, and hashes every
regular file against the published integrity manifest. It does not mutate
metadata or repoint the activation symlink.

`manifest-rollback` validates and selects the exact existing generation derived
from the manifest's current lock. It accepts neither a package cache nor a
shared package root and never extracts artifacts.

Runtime scaffold targets are currently materialized in the same immutable
generation as package payload files and are included in the compound projection
key. The runtime creates mount targets and writable tmpfs overlays after
pivoting into the read-only generation, but activation descriptors are not yet
separate from payload storage. Payload/scaffold separation remains roadmap
Phase C; policy-only scaffold changes can still duplicate package payload data.

`manifest-generations` reports selected, pinned, leased, and retained generations
with allocated bytes. `manifest-pin` and `manifest-unpin` maintain explicit
retention markers outside immutable generation roots and coordinate with
activation. Every package-manifest launch acquires a shared kernel lock for the
exact selected generation before compact admission continues. The lock survives
the supervisor forks and closes automatically when the launch exits or crashes;
a persistent lease marker by itself is not active state.

`manifest-gc` deletes unselected, unpinned, and inactive generations by default.
It holds the activation lock and rechecks selection, pin, and lease state before
each deletion. `--dry-run` reports the same candidates without deleting them.
Inactive lease marker files are removed after an exclusive nonblocking lock
proves that no launch still holds the marker inode.
The Signal Desktop manifest is Wayland-oriented and declares the audio/media and
session bus sockets needed for a usable desktop app; Wayland screensharing still
depends on an appropriate host portal service reachable through the declared
session bus.

`examples/arch-dev.toml` uses `/home/user/.local/state/containermanager/roots/arch`
and a separate mutable prefix at
`/home/user/.local/state/containermanager/prefixes/microkernel`; `/home/dev`
and `/shared` inside the guest are prefix-local bind mounts. The profile policy
includes only the per-socket desktop integrations it declares; the example
profile declares Wayland and PipeWire and leaves PulseAudio-compatible access
denied.

`examples/firefox-nightly.toml` is the first app profile. It expects
`firefox-nightly-bin` staged in the Arch root at `/usr/lib/firefox-nightly` with
`/usr/bin/firefox-nightly` as the entrypoint, and uses an isolated prefix at
`/home/user/.local/state/containermanager/prefixes/firefox-nightly`. The profile
declares Wayland, PipeWire, PulseAudio-compatible audio, raw session D-Bus, and
one DRI render device for GPU rendering. It also declares Firefox-specific
Wayland environment variables without inheriting the host environment. System
D-Bus stays denied. A bounded real-workload smoke writes into the isolated guest
home:

```sh
timeout 30s cargo run -- run --profile examples/firefox-nightly.toml -- \
  /usr/bin/firefox-nightly --headless --screenshot /home/firefox/firefox-nightly-example.png https://example.com
```

A visible launch uses the profile's default Firefox entrypoint:

```sh
cargo run -- run --profile examples/firefox-nightly.toml
```

For the current host GPU, the Arch root also has `vulkan-radeon` installed so
Mesa can use RADV through the declared render node. The example render-device
source is this host's udev by-path symlink,
`/dev/dri/by-path/pci-0000:0c:00.0-render`; update that value for a different
GPU rather than binding all of `/dev/dri`.

The `probe-root` command opens the fast `open_tree(OPEN_TREE_NAMESPACE)` path
and prints the returned fd; it does not enter the namespace or exec a command.
`--userns` maps container uid/gid 0 to the caller before probing, which is the
expected rootless path.

The `probe-idmap` command creates a detached tmpfs, applies `MOUNT_ATTR_IDMAP`,
enters a cloned root namespace, attaches the idmapped tmpfs at `/tmp`, writes a
probe file there, and prints the observed uid/gid. The attachment is isolated to
the probe process. The second `probe-idmap` example maps the rootless caller to
guest uid/gid `1000` and proves that the idmapped mount reports and creates
files as `1000:1000`.

`probe-attach --idmap` exercises idmapping on cloned host binds. Rootless host
bind idmapping may be denied by the kernel because host filesystems are mounted
in the initial user namespace. To model a prefix owned on the host by a
different uid/gid, pass `--namespace-uid 1000 --namespace-gid 1000 --parent-uid
1001 --parent-gid 1001`; creating that map requires the corresponding
UID/GID-mapping privileges, and idmapping a host filesystem bind also requires
`CAP_SYS_ADMIN` where that filesystem was mounted.

The default Arch development profile leaves host-bind idmapping disabled
because this host denies rootless idmapped binds for filesystems mounted in the
initial user namespace. The idmap probes are kept to validate kernel behavior
and future privileged/idmapped mount paths.

